      "id": "combien-futur-prendre",
      "title": "Combien ? Le futur proche et prendre",
      "minutes": 9,
      "lede": "Three tools for shopping and planning: asking the price with combien, announcing what you are going to do with aller, and the irregular verb prendre.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Combien ?",
          "headers": [
            "Question",
            "Réponse"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Combien coûte un melon ?",
              "Un melon coûte deux euros cinquante."
            ],
            [
              "Combien coûtent les cerises ?",
              "Elles coûtent neuf euros le kilo."
            ],
            [
              "C'est combien ?",
              "C'est trois euros vingt."
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Le futur proche : aller + infinitif",
          "headers": [
            "Forme",
            "Exemple"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "affirmative",
              "Je vais commander."
            ],
            [
              "négative",
              "Je ne vais pas cuisiner."
            ],
            [
              "question",
              "Qu'est-ce qu'on va faire samedi pour le dîner ?"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Prendre",
          "headers": [
            "Sujet",
            "Au présent"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "je",
              "prends"
            ],
            [
              "tu",
              "prends"
            ],
            [
              "il / elle",
              "prend"
            ],
            [
              "nous",
              "prenons"
            ],
            [
              "vous",
              "prenez"
            ],
            [
              "ils / elles",
              "prennent"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Gotchas",
          "text": "- Coûter agrees with the thing bought: Combien coûte un melon ? (singular) but Combien coûtent les fraises ? (plural). The question with C'est combien ? works for anything.\n- Futur proche = “going to”: Je vais acheter du pain. The aller is conjugated and the next verb stays in the infinitive. Use it for tomorrow, tonight, next week, and anything you plan.\n- Negation goes around aller only: Je ne vais pas cuisiner. (not “je vais ne pas cuisiner”). Same pattern as the other verbs you know: Il ne va pas venir.\n- Aller alone means “to go”: Je vais au marché. Aller + infinitive means “going to do”: Je vais acheter des fruits. Both can appear: Je vais aller au marché.\n- Prendre: the d disappears in the singular and returns in the plural, and ils prennent doubles the n (the e is silent, nn gives the plural away). Same pattern for apprendre, comprendre.\n- Prendre also means to eat or drink: Je prends un café. Je prends du fromage. In a shop, Je prends ça = I'll take that."
        },
