    {
      "id": "sons-o-on",
      "title": "Les sons [o] et [ɔ̃]",
      "minutes": 4,
      "lede": "Two vowel sounds that look alike: [o] is oral (the air goes through the mouth), [ɔ̃] is nasal (the air also goes through the nose).",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Oral ou nasal ?",
          "headers": [
            "Son",
            "Exemples"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "[o] oral",
              "le kilo · la pomme · d'accord · la tomate · Locavor"
            ],
            [
              "[ɔ̃] nasal",
              "le melon · le thon · elles sont · nous voudrions · non · on"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "How to hear it",
          "text": "- For [ɔ̃], round your lips and let the air go through your nose. The n is not pronounced: melon sounds me-lõ, not me-lon.\n- Be careful with the letter o followed by n: when n is followed by a vowel, the sound isn't nasal (une, bonne, donne, personne: bɔn, dɔn). Compare bon [bɔ̃] / bonne [bɔn].\n- The final -t of le thon is silent, and so is the final -s in nous sommes. Compare le thon [tɔ̃] with la tonne [tɔn]."
        },
        {
